 a sound, and standard choice, given it's a common and solid choice for Black's first move, and keeps things centered. This is the optimal move from a practical and convention perspective.  And I'll stick with (F,5) to stick with the typical line used in literature.\n\nThis is a straight-forward choice. All moves being isomorphic, it becomes about picking a consistent option.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Situation: The game has just begun, and the board is in the standard starting configuration. I am playing as \'*\' (Black), and the opponent is \'@\' (White). The board displays diagonal symmetry. The available moves are (C, 4), (D, 3), (E, 6), and (F, 5).\n\nEvaluation: Due to the symmetry of the initial board state, all four valid moves are strategically equivalent.\n- Moves (C, 4) and (D, 3) flip the opponent\'s piece at D4.\n- Moves (E, 6) and (F, 5) flip the opponent\'s piece at E5.\nPlaying any of these moves results in a board shape that is a rotation or reflection of the others. There is no advantage to one specific move over the others at this stage.\n\nReason: Since all options are theoretically equal, I will choose (F, 5). This is a standard opening move (a "perpendicular" cut) that flips the opponent\'s piece at E5 vertically, maintaining a centralized group of pieces without exposing any edges or corners.\n\nAnswer: (F, 5)'}
