their mobility low. It is safe and does not expose any corners.\n- **(A, 5)**: This is also a quiet move that flips only one disc (B4). However, it places a piece on the edge (A-row) without securing the corner. While acceptable, central moves are generally preferred at this stage unless the edge move is strategic.\n- **(F, 4)**: This move flips three discs (E4, D4, E3). While it takes a central square, flipping many internal discs creates a large block of my color, which typically gives the opponent many attacking options on the perimeter.\n- **(G, 1) and (G, 7)**: These are C-squares and X-squares relative to empty corners. (G, 1) allows the opponent to take H1, and (G, 7) allows the opponent to take H8 immediately. These are blunders.\n\nReason: I choose **(B, 3)** because it is the most effective "quiet move" available. It flips a single disc, maintaining a tight board position and limiting the opponent\'s counter-play options. It avoids the risks associated with edge play or opening up corners.\n\nAnswer: (B, 3)'}
