*, **(G, 1)** are C-squares, and **(G, 7)** is an X-square. Playing these squares adjacent to corners often allows the opponent to force a capture of the corner, so they should be avoided.\n- **(B, 6)**, **(D, 7)**, **(E, 7)**, and **(F, 7)** are moves that extend towards the outer edges. While valid, they tend to open up the board and increase the opponent\'s mobility.\n- **(F, 3)** and **(F, 4)** are moves within the central 4x4 "sweet 16" square. These are generally safer.\n- **(F, 3)** flips the vertical line D3 and E3. This connects the isolated piece at F2 to the main group at C3/D3/E3, forming a solid block.\n- **(F, 4)** flips D4 and E4. While good, it leaves a gap at F3 between the piece at F2 and the new piece.\n\nReason: I chose **(F, 3)** because it is a quiet, central move that maximizes stability. By flipping D3 and E3, I consolidate my pieces on the left side of the board and connect F2 to the group, minimizing the frontier exposed to the opponent. It avoids risky edge squares and limits the opponent\'s counter-play options.\n\nAnswer: (F, 3)'}
