lready hold C1, and C8 is an edge piece, this effectively secures the entire row. The pieces in row C become very difficult for the opponent to flip back, acting as a stable wall. It does not immediately give the opponent access to the adjacent corners (A8 or H8) because the neighboring squares B8 and D8 are not easily playable for them.\n2.  **(B, 2)**: This is a safe, quiet move that fills a gap in the B-row and flips vertical pieces in the C and D rows. It strengthens my position in the top-left sector but doesn't yield the massive stable gain of C8.\n3.  **(A, 6)**: Extends the A-edge control. While good, it is not as decisive as taking the full C-row.\n\nReason: The move (C, 8) is the strongest because it secures a full edge (Row C) by utilizing the existing anchor at C1. This flips 6 opponent pieces into a stable state (horizontally unflippable) and creates a significant territory advantage. It limits the opponent's options on the right side of the board without exposing critical weaknesses like corners.\n\nAnswer: (C, 8)"}
