ces at H3, H4, and H5 to form a solid wall (H3-H6). It captures opponent pieces at G6 and F6 vertically. It is a safe move that does not give the opponent access to the H8 corner.\n- **(H, 7)**: This move flips a large number of pieces diagonally (from B1 to G6). However, it places a piece on a "C-square" (adjacent to the H8 corner). Since I do not control the corner yet, this is risky and could allow the opponent to force a corner capture later.\n- **(G, 5)**: A solid internal move that captures pieces in the center (F4, F5, F6). It is a good alternative but does not offer the strategic stability of the edge move at H6.\n- **(F, 7)**: This move captures G6 but places a piece in a weak position adjacent to the opponent\'s wall and empty spaces, potentially giving the initiative to the opponent.\n\nReason: (H, 6) is the best choice because it builds stable territory on the bottom edge. By securing H6, I restrict the opponent\'s access to the bottom-right quadrant and flip their pieces at F6 and G6, reducing their influence. Unlike (H, 7), it does not risk giving away the corner.\n\nAnswer: (H, 6)'}
