e game for a better piece count. This, in the grand scheme, appears to be the most promising outcome.\n\n\n", 'output': "Situation: The game is in the endgame phase with only 5 empty squares remaining (G1, G2, G8, H1, H2). The most critical feature of the current board is the empty corner at H1. Moves adjacent to this corner (G1, G2, H2) must be evaluated carefully to avoid giving the opponent access to the corner, which creates stable discs and usually leads to winning the edge.\n\nEvaluation:\n1.  **Move (G, 2)**: This is an X-square move (diagonal to the corner). If you play here, you place a piece at G2. The opponent has a piece at F3. This creates a diagonal line H1 (empty) -> G2 (*) -> F3 (@). The opponent can immediately play at H1, capturing the corner and flipping your piece. This is a bad move.\n2.  **Move (H, 2)**: This is a C-square move (adjacent to the corner along the edge). If you play here, row H becomes H1 (.) H2 (*) H3 (*) ... H7 (@). The opponent can immediately play at H1, sandwiching your pieces between H1 and H7, flipping almost the entire bottom row to their color. This is a disastrous move.\n3.  **Move (G, 1)**: This is also a C-square move. However, playing here flips the opponent's piece at F1 to your color (*). After this move, the configuration along column 1 is H1 (.) G1 (*) F1 (*). Because F1 is now your piece, the opponent cannot use it to sandwich your piece at G1 from H1. There are no other opponent pieces enabling a capture of H1 (H2 and G2 are empty). Therefore, this move does *not* give the corner to the opponent.\n\nReason: Move (G, 1) is the only move that prevents the opponent from immediately capturing the valuable H1 corner. Both (G, 2) and (H, 2) allow the opponent to take the corner on their next turn, leading to significant material loss. (G, 1) secures the left edge and forces the opponent into a confined game path (likely G8) where you can maintain your advantage.\n\nAnswer: (G, 1)"}
